---
const places = [
  ["before", "before breakfast", "Earlier than breakfast"],
  ["after", "after school", "Later than school"],
  ["during", "during the concert", "Within the time of the concert"],
  ["beside", "beside the river", "Next to the river"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Connecting Ideas - Prepositions</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-green">
    <h2>Before a noun phrase</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Word</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          places.map(([word, example, meaning]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{word}</th>
              <td>{example}</td>
              <td>{meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Beside</strong> means next to: “The bag is <strong>beside</strong> the chair.” <strong>Besides</strong> can mean in addition: “<strong>Besides</strong> tea, we have juice.”</p>
    <p>“<strong>During the concert</strong>” is followed by a noun phrase. “<strong>While we listened</strong>” is followed by a clause. Say “while we listened”, not “during we listened”.</p>
    <p>Other place phrases: <em>above the door, beyond the bridge, across the field</em>. “We found a café <strong>nearby</strong>” uses <em>nearby</em> as a place adverb.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>The same outing, three claims</h2>
    <p>“We left <strong>because the show ended</strong>” gives a reason. “The show ended. <strong>Afterwards</strong>, we left” shows sequence. “We left <strong>after the show</strong>” shows time with a noun phrase.</p>
    <p>Something happening after another event does not automatically mean it happened because of that event.</p>
    <p>To choose a connector: name the relationship, check what follows, read the sentence, then test another word.</p>
    <p>“We stayed indoors _____ the heavy rain.” <em>The heavy rain</em> is a noun phrase. <strong>During</strong> tells when. <strong>Because of</strong> tells why. <em>Because the heavy rain</em> is incomplete. <em>Because</em> needs a clause, such as <em>because it was raining heavily</em>.</p>
  </section>
</div>
